Efficiency-only review of the uncommitted diff (scripts/db.py new adversarial-pass functions, scripts/dbcore.py additions, scripts/audit/adversarial_pass_audit.py, scripts/audit/supersession_backpointer_audit.py, scripts/audit/research_tooling_separation.py, scripts/audit/derived_not_curated_audit.py). Repo scale confirmed: 81 tables, 1038 columns total (`PRAGMA table_info` summed).

```json
[
  {
    "file": "scripts/audit/derived_not_curated_audit.py",
    "line": 333,
    "summary": "scan_numeric_mirrors() re-walks the entire schema (SELECT sqlite_master + PRAGMA table_info for all 81 tables) independently of the pre-existing check_vocabularies() and scan_writer_columns(), which already do the same table x column walk in main(); and for every one of the ~1038 columns it calls dbcore.check_expression(), which itself re-fetches and re-parses that table's whole CREATE TABLE DDL from sqlite_master on every single call, so a table with N columns has its DDL text fetched+regex-parsed N times just inside this one function (and again N times inside check_vocabularies' per-column check_values() calls).",
    "cheaper_alternative": "Do one schema pass per run: fetch (table, sql) once per table from sqlite_master, PRAGMA table_info once per table, and derive the column-name set, the CHECK value-set (Class 1/3), and the CHECK expression (Class 4/'between') all from that single DDL parse. Thread that one {table: {col: (values, expr)}} structure into check_vocabularies, scan_writer_columns and scan_numeric_mirrors instead of each re-querying sqlite_master and PRAGMA table_info from scratch, and only call the BETWEEN-extraction logic for INTEGER columns (skip columns already resolved by check_values, since a column can't be both an IN-list and a BETWEEN)."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/db.py",
    "line": 4093,
    "summary": "record_adversarial_pass() opens and fully parses the reviewer transcript JSONL twice: _transcript_models(reviewer_path) (line 4093) streams every line and json.loads()s each one to collect models, then _last_assistant_text(reviewer_path) (line 4099) opens the same file again and streams+json.loads()s every line a second time to find the last assistant text/SubagentHandback call. Transcripts are full session logs and can be large.",
    "cheaper_alternative": "Merge _transcript_models and _last_assistant_text into a single-pass reader that, while iterating the file once, both accumulates the distinct assistant-turn models and tracks the last text/handback content, returning both from one open()/json.loads() sweep."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/audit/research_tooling_separation.py",
    "line": 115,
    "summary": "classify_path() is called once per changed path inside the `for p in paths` loop, and each call invokes run_checks.classify([path], reg), which recompiles every kind's glob patterns via glob_to_re() from scratch on every call (run_checks.py line 128). For a changeset of N files this recompiles all kind regexes N times instead of once.",
    "cheaper_alternative": "Call run_checks.classify(paths, reg) once for the whole changeset (it already loops over every path internally) and read the per-kind path lists out of its returned attribution dict to derive each path's research/tooling flags, instead of re-invoking classify() per single path."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/audit/supersession_backpointer_audit.py",
    "line": 125,
    "summary": "LEDGER (references/project-standards.md) is read from disk twice: once inside the `for src in sources: text = src.read_text()` loop at line 108 (sources includes LEDGER), and again explicitly via `ledger_text = LEDGER.read_text(...)` at line 125.",
    "cheaper_alternative": "Capture the ledger's text in a dict keyed by path during the first read loop (or special-case LEDGER there) and reuse that string for ledger_prose, instead of reading the same file a second time."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/audit/supersession_backpointer_audit.py",
    "line": 135,
    "summary": "Inside the `for src, target_rel, ... in entries` loop, target_file.read_text() (line 135) and _paragraphs(target_text) (line 154) are recomputed independently for every SUPERSEDES entry, even when multiple entries (e.g. from different DR files) name the same target document (CLAUDE.md / project-standards.md are the two most-cited targets by the module's own docstring). Each repeat re-reads the file and re-splits/re-normalises every line.",
    "cheaper_alternative": "Memoize read_text() and _paragraphs() results per target_rel path (a dict populated on first use) so a target file shared by several SUPERSEDES entries is read and parsed once, not once per citing entry."
  }
]
```

Notes on scope/exclusions: scripts/audit/adversarial_pass_audit.py, scripts/audit/derived_not_curated_audit.py's scan_module_collections and scan_comment_vocabularies, and scripts/dbcore.py's new normalise_ledger_quote/count_ledger_quote/strip_supersedes_citations/schema_range functions were reviewed and found to do single-pass or small-N work appropriate to this repo's scale (a handful of tables/files/findings) — no redundant I/O or O(n*m) restructuring opportunity worth flagging there. No closure/captured-scope objects were found outliving their need; dbcore.py's `_CHOICES_CONNS` module-level connection cache is an intentional, documented per-process cache, not a stray closure.

